why did we choose docx and not latex and why this template?
Weighed ATS compatibility and practical usability factors

A few concrete reasons, mostly tied back to what we found in the ATS research:

Why .docx over LaTeX:

    ATS parsing reliability. LaTeX's output is still just a PDF at the end, and the popular LaTeX resume templates (Awesome-CV, Deedy-Resume, etc.) lean heavily on multi-column layouts, custom fonts, and icon-based section markers — exactly the pattern our ATS report flagged as breaking parsers (columns get read interleaved, icons/graphics get dropped entirely, non-standard fonts sometimes don't extract as real text). A hand-rolled docx sidesteps that by construction.
    Taleo/iCIMS specifically. Our own research found these older, still-common enterprise ATS platforms parse .docx more reliably than PDF — LaTeX only outputs PDF, so it can't take advantage of that at all.
    Editability. You can open a .docx in Word or Google Docs and tweak a line yourself in ten seconds. A LaTeX resume requires you to either come back to me or maintain a LaTeX toolchain locally just to change a date.
    Real hyperlinks. docx (OOXML) stores hyperlinks as standard relationships that every modern ATS parses as plain visible text if the link itself gets stripped — same category of reliability, easier to guarantee than chasing down the right LaTeX package's hyperlink behavior.

Why this specific template:
It's basically the distilled version of every finding from the reports we did — not a stylistic choice so much as "boring on purpose": single column, standard section headers (Education/Experience/Projects/Skills), reverse-chronological, one consistent font (Calibri), no graphics/icons/tables, quantified bullets. That's the exact shape the r/EngineeringResumes wiki, ResumeWorded's own rubric, and the ATS parsing tests all converged on independently. There wasn't a fancier design competing for the slot — the boring template is the optimized one for this use case.

If you specifically want a LaTeX version for some other reason (e.g. a portfolio site, or you just prefer the typographic control), I can build one — just flagging it'd be a step down on ATS-safety unless we keep it single-column and icon-free too, in which case LaTeX vs. docx stops mattering much either way.most job applications look for a pdf
